<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zagros Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zagros</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zagros Mountains are a long mountain range in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, stretching from the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and separating the Mesopotamian plain from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. Their highland peoples repeatedly pressed on Mesopotamian states, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>. The Zagros was the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, and its tribes, including the Zand and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lak">Lak</a>, formed the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains.jpg" alt="Zagros Mountains" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Terpsichores · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ecoregion_PA0446.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Zagros range is 1,600 km long and spans <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. It begins in north-western Iran, follows Iran's western border, and runs south-east to end at the Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>. Its highest point is Mount Dena, at 4,409 metres. The mountains were formed mainly by the collision of the Eurasian and Arabian plates, chiefly during the Miocene, and the collision continues. They are entirely of sedimentary origin, made mainly of limestone, and are divided into many parallel sub-ranges. Salt domes are common, and Iran's main oilfields lie in the western central foothills.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Humans have lived in the Zagros since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-paleolithic">Lower Paleolithic</a>. The earliest human fossils found there are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthal</a>, from Shanidar Cave, Bisitun Cave and Wezmeh Cave, with the remains of ten Neanderthals at Shanidar dating from about 65,000 to 35,000 years ago. Signs of early agriculture in the foothills go back as far as 9000 BCE, and some settlements later grew into cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a> and Susa. Wine storage from between 3500 and 5400 BCE has been found at Hajji Firuz Tepe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godin-tepe">Godin Tepe</a>, among the earliest evidence of wine production.</p><p>In ancient times the Zagros was the home of peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrians">Hurrians</a>, Guti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a>, Turukku and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lullubi">Lullubi</a>, who periodically invaded the Sumerian, Akkadian and Assyrian cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. The mountains form a geographic barrier between the Mesopotamian plain and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. A small archive of clay tablets from Tell Shemshara on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-zab">Little Zab</a> records the interactions of these groups in the early second millennium BCE. Ancient Mesopotamians placed the entrance to the underworld in the Zagros, with a staircase leading down to its gates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Zagros has long been home to pastoralists and farmers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lurs">Lurs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and Qashqai, who move their herds from the eastern slopes in summer to the western slopes in winter. Cities at the foothills include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaymaniyah">Sulaymaniyah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramabad">Khorramabad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>. Oak woodland and pistachio and almond steppe remain, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/overgrazing">overgrazing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">deforestation</a> have degraded them, and the ancestors of wheat, barley, lentil, almond, pistachio, pomegranate and grape grow wild there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zagros Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The military threat came from the Gutians, a highland people from the <b>Zagros</b> mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Territorial ambition first appeared under the Amorite conqueror Shamshi-Adad I (r. c. 1808–1776 BCE), who seized Assur, styled himself &quot;King of the Universe&quot; and built a kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia stretching from the <b>Zagros</b> foothills to the Euphrates and Mari.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1217 Muhammad II marched on Baghdad against the Abbasid caliph, but the campaign was abandoned when snow overwhelmed his army in the <b>Zagros</b> mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Ishpuini and grandson Menua began writing in Urartian, a language related to Hurrian, using a cuneiform script adapted from Assyria, and extended royal power to the religious centre of Musasir in the <b>Zagros</b>, to the lands north of Lake Van and towards Lake Urmia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Median Empire grew in the 7th century BCE out of a confederation of tribes in the <b>Zagros Mountains</b>, many of whose chiefdoms had long paid tribute to Assyria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Shulgi, the army campaigned repeatedly in the <b>Zagros</b> against peoples such as the Lullubi and the city of Simurrum, while Susa and parts of Elam were brought under Ur’s control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
